Using bsdutils in place of GNU coreutils does come with some
tradeoffs.  The list below highlights some of these that you should
consider before making the switch:


Some commands do not exist
--------------------------

The following commands are specific to GNU coreutils and are not part
of a BSD base system but have alternatives:

    COMMAND    DESCRIPTION                    ALTERNATIVE
    arch       print machine arch name        uname -m
    dir        list directory contents        ls
    dircolors  color setup for ls             ls supports color (-G)
    vdir       vertical 'dir' command         ls
    hostid     print numeric host identifier  none - dropped in 4.4BSD

The following commands are specific to GNU coreutils and have no
alternative provided by a BSD system:

    COMMAND    DESCRIPTION
    pinky      lightweight finger


Lack of long options
--------------------

This is a usability thing, but if you have a lot of scripts that use
the long options for GNU coreutils commands, you will have to change
those to short options with bsdutils.  Keep in mind the short options
often vary between GNU and BSD commands.  See each command's man page
for more details.


No support for SELinux
----------------------

The ls(1) command from GNU coreutils can display SELinux information
in file listings.  Since this is Linux-specific, the bsdutils do not
have support for it.  If you don't need this or don't care about
SELinux, then the bsdutils should be functionally equivalent for you.

Some commands have FreeBSD mac.h functionality which is sort of like
SELinux on Linux.  Those commands have had the mac.h functionality
removed, but they would be an ideal starting point for flipping that
over to SELinux on Linux systems.


No translations
---------------

The bsdutils commands come with very simple untranslated help output
and an English language man page.  There may be translated man pages
in the FreeBSD source tree (we should look for those), but the
commands themselves don't output text in other languages.  However,
text manipulation commands and things like ls(1) do work with UTF-8
encodings and wide characters.


date(1) option semantics
------------------------

The date(1) in cragutils keeps the FreeBSD options, and they differ
fundamentally from GNU date's (tests/diff cases date/r-epoch,
date/f-parse, date/jur, date/jf-format):

    -r EPOCH    print the time from seconds-since-epoch (GNU: -r is a
                reference FILE)
    -j -f FMT   parse and display without setting the clock (GNU has
                no -j; -f means input FILE)
    -jur EPOCH  shorthand for the same (GNU: invalid options)

GNU spellings like `date -d @1000000000` do not exist here; scripts
ported from GNU environments must switch to the BSD forms.  Two notes
from the corpus work:

  - cragutils date -r also accepts a file name and then prints from its
    mtime (case date/r-file matches GNU exactly) — a compatibility
    extension over stock FreeBSD.
  - `date -f FMT STRING` *without* -j sets the clock from the parsed
    string (it fails with "clock_settime: Operation not permitted" when
    unprivileged, but succeeds as root).  The probe in firstboot.sh
    (`date -u -f %s "$floor"`) therefore sets the system time as a side
    effect when run as root; use -j for display-only parsing.


find(1) has no -newermt
-----------------------

GNU find's `-newermt ABSOLUTE-TIME` is not supported; the tool rejects
it with an error and exit 1 (case find/newermt) where GNU lists the
matching paths.  FreeBSD find's spelling is the -newerXY family.  Note
that the initramfs-tools portability patch in cports
(0005-general-portability-fixes-for-bsdutils-and-musl.patch) invokes
`find -newermt`, which fails against this find(1).


patch(1) progress reporting and failure handling
------------------------------------------------

Happy-path application, dry runs, reverse application and the
"previously applied" case behave identically to GNU patch — same exit
status and same resulting files (cases patch/p1-apply, patch/p1-dryrun,
patch/p1-reverse, patch/p1-already, patch/ctx-p0).  The stdout wording
differs ("Hmm... Looks like a unified diff to me... / Patching file X
using Plan A..." vs. GNU's "patching file X") which the harness flags
as a divergence; scripts must not parse patch(1) output.

On a patch that goes bad partway through (case patch/bad-hunk) the
behavior differs materially: cragutils applies the good hunks, keeps
the file partially patched, writes .orig and .rej files and reports on
stdout with exit 1; GNU patch aborts on the malformed hunk, leaves the
file unmodified with no rejects, reports on stderr and exits 2.  Build
automation that relies on "a failed patch leaves the tree untouched"
(as GNU guarantees) must instead check for .rej files or probe with
--dry-run first (build/lib/kernel.sh already probes with --dry-run).


sed -i requires an extension argument
-------------------------------------

BSD sed's -i takes the backup extension as its argument; GNU-style
`sed -i 's/.../.../' file` (case sed/i-inplace) does not edit the file
and exits 1 where GNU edits in place and exits 0.  Use the attached
extension form `sed -i.bak ...` (case sed/i-suffix — identical to
GNU).  The image hooks 10-create-users.sh and 35-dev-console-login.sh
use the GNU spelling; they run in the GNU build container today and
would silently stop editing if run against the target userland.


sh echo does not interpret backslash escapes
--------------------------------------------

The echo builtin of cragutils sh prints `a\tb` literally (case
sh/echo-escape) where dash's echo expands the escape to a tab.  Use
printf(1) when escapes are wanted; note the direction: the permissive
behavior is dash's, so scripts relying on it break on the target.


stat(1) is BSD stat, not GNU coreutils stat
-------------------------------------------

`stat -c FORMAT` is a GNU-ism: cragutils rejects it with exit 1 (cases
stat/c-perm, stat/c-size) where GNU prints the mode/size.  Conversely
`stat -f FORMAT` is the BSD format string (cases stat/f-perm,
stat/f-size, stat/basic, stat/f-name all print the requested fields)
where GNU's -f reports file-system information.  The Crag image hooks
use `stat -c %a` (05-platform-users.sh, 10-create-users.sh,
35-dev-console-login.sh); those run in the GNU build container, but
target-side scripts must use BSD formats (as the cports bubblewrap
tests patch already does with `stat -f '%OLp'`).


jot(1) counts to infinity on a zero repetition count
----------------------------------------------------

`jot 0` is not "print nothing": BSD jot treats a zero repetition count
as infinity and counts 1, 2, 3, ... forever (reps == 0 sets the
infinity flag in jot.c), where GNU `seq 0` prints nothing.  The
differential corpus deliberately has no `jot 0` case (unbounded output
is not byte-comparable); tests/t/12-regress-tools.sh pins the infinity
semantics with `jot 0 | head -3`.  Scripts that want a bounded count of
zero items must test the count before invoking jot.


sh(1) echo prints backslash escapes literally
---------------------------------------------

The FreeBSD shell's echo builtin does not interpret backslash escapes:
`echo "a\tb"` prints the five characters a \ t b (case sh/echo-escape,
identical in cragutils and the FreeBSD oracle), while dash's echo (the
host's /usr/bin/sh) expands \t to a tab.  bash in POSIX mode agrees
with the BSD behavior.  Do not rely on either expansion in portable
scripts; use `printf '%s\n'` when the escapes must be interpreted.
